The brief's SPECIFICATION EXPORT: a document carrying the floor, species,
finish, pattern, width, area, estimated range and project id, that a visitor
can print or hand to a spouse, a designer, a realtor or their own
contractor. Program 1 lists it under design collaboration.

IT REUSES THE SHEET THAT ALREADY EXISTS

/design/spec has rendered a printable Ecowoods floor specification since the
configurator shipped. Building a studio-flavoured copy of it is precisely
the duplication Rule #5 forbids, so the sheet now reads a Floor Studio share
code as well as a /design configuration and one document serves both. The
studio gains a "Print the specification" exit; nothing else moves.

A studio design fills rows a DesignConfig structurally cannot hold: the
board width, the reference and design id, which band set priced it, what the
visitor asked the floor to feel like, and what their photo read as. The room
line is a READING — three words the browser measured — never a photograph,
because Floor Studio retains none and the sheet has no image to show.

THE CURRENCY BUG THIS WOULD HAVE CREATED

The sheet formatted Canadian dollars unconditionally. That was correct for
as long as it only ever saw a /design configuration, and would have printed
an Ontario figure on a specification a Buffalo homeowner was about to hand a
contractor — the exact mistake GEO-006 exists to prevent, arriving through a
door GEO-006 never had to guard.

It now prices through `priceConfiguration` with the design's own country and
formats by `estimate.currency`. The EstimateResult fields still read `…Cad`,
a naming debt from before New York bands existed, and a formatter that
trusted the field name would print the right number with the wrong symbol —
worse than either mistake alone. A test asserts the two currencies produce
different figures rather than assuming it.

THE HELPER THAT WAS LYING

MEAS-04 added a comment-stripper to design-config.test.ts so "no hand-built
quote link" would not match the docblock that explains the bug. Right
instinct, wrong tool. Measured on this repository, the same regex removes
THIRTEEN real statements from FloorStudio.tsx — a regex is not a TypeScript
lexer, and an over-eager one turns every `not.toContain` into a test that
passes because the code it was searching for was deleted before the search.

I found it because two SALE-02 assertions failed against code I had just
written and could see in the file.

Scope, stated precisely rather than dramatically: exactly ONE shipped test
file carries it — design-config.test.ts, from d6abf24. Its own assertions
were not actually compromised, because the two files it strips lose a single
semicolon each and the one file that loses thirteen statements is read only
by a POSITIVE assertion, where over-stripping causes a false failure rather
than a false pass. The helper was unsound; its results happened to be
correct. That is not a distinction worth relying on twice.

So the stripper is gone, and the assertions are positive and code-shaped
instead: `const quoteHref = designEstimateHref(` and `href={quoteHref}` are
both what we actually require and something prose cannot accidentally
satisfy. Where a negative assertion is still right it reads raw source, so
the failure mode is a FALSE FAILURE — which gets noticed — rather than a
false pass, which does not. instrumentation.test.ts never had the helper and
is unchanged.

The share code is itself a querystring, so `specHref` percent-encodes it;
dropped in raw, everything after its first `&` would become a sibling
parameter and the design would arrive as its first field only. Tested.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards (verify:css needs postcss from node_modules,
absent here). parse-scan 613 files, zero diagnostics. Resolving typecheck
clean across every changed file. 26 new assertions; 68,255 across the local
suite.

